## The name step, which is an open owner decision

The ADR's first open decision is what a bind by name alone becomes. Its recommended option is built here so that it is measured, and it is one linker to remove or change: a call nothing else resolved is bound to the one method (a call on a receiver) or function (a bare call) of that name, marked `name`, at 0.7; several holders, unresolved. It runs only for a file whose analyzer screens the names of the language's own library: JS/TS and Rust do, C# does not, so C# has no name bind.

Why it exists: the first JS/TS and Rust adapters deleted every name bind, and resolved calls fell to 11,092 and 4,768, with 13 and 10 of 15 sampled lost edges right. Measured now: JS/TS 7,757 binds restored to their earlier target, 14 right of 15 read; Rust 1,549 restored, 13 of 15; the 14 Rust binds with no earlier bind of that name, 5 right (standard-library names its list lacks: filed).

## Known, not done (each filed in the tracker with its count)

Overload selection; a C# base class written with type arguments; partial-type ancestors (2 wrong `base.M()` edges on Dapper); a qualifier that is a member named like its type; a C# screen of library names; Rust standard-library names, constructors called by name, wildcards of inline modules; `class_hint` as a reason to bind; a deduplicated call left as an open stub; an interface-typed TypeScript call now names the interface member and not its one implementation. With the rust-analyzer backend on, a call both arms agree on is corroborated at the producer merge, which runs before the call linkers: calls the linkers bind are no longer corroborated (on the recorded crate 94 corroborated edges became 55).

